## Problem

On native Windows, a normal chat run can terminate the API process when `SessionStore.writeArray` cannot rename a temporary state file over an existing `session-runs/<session-id>.json`. The rejected persistence operation escapes and Node exits; clients then receive `ECONNREFUSED`, and the stack supervisor cleans up the remaining services.

Confirmed by running **upstream main**, not only a downstream branch:
- Commit: `eae2cd354a366dab3796f46c9da8fe5dd7aff11c`.
- Windows 11 Pro, build 26200, x64; native Node.js 22.23.4; pnpm 11.1.2.
- Clean detached worktree; independent `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` and `pnpm build`; no product-source or lockfile changes. Startup reported Runner build `eae2cd35`.
- Native executor, real API and Runner, real managed Bash execution; a deterministic local OpenAI-compatible SSE server supplies model tool calls. No paid model, mocked filesystem, artificial lock holder, or fault injection.
- Existing managed environment and offline package cache were reused in an isolated data directory. No user data was modified.

## Reproduction

1. Check out the commit above in a native Windows checkout, install locked dependencies, and build.
2. Prepare a working managed Python/Bash starter environment through product setup. Use isolated data and ports. Existing local package caches can be reused.
3. Start the product with `node scripts/start-stack.mjs --mode local --no-build --no-jiuwenswarm`. For the client below, use API port 24410 and free model-stub port 24420; point `SCIENCE_AGENT_RUNNER_URL` at the matching isolated Runner port. Set a test-owned `SCIENCE_AGENT_AUTH_TOKEN`.
4. Save the client below as `reproduce.mjs`, set `REPRO_TOKEN` to that same test token, then run `node reproduce.mjs`. It registers a loopback model, creates sessions, submits real chat requests, polls run state, and retrieves execution output. All filesystem operations are product operations.
5. In one continuous upstream-main attempt, **10 runs completed successfully; run 11 crashed the API**. This is timing-sensitive, not a claim that every machine will fail on run 11. The prior downstream test also encountered the same failure naturally.

<details><summary>Executable reproduction client</summary>

```js
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import {createServer} from 'node:http';
import {writeFile} from 'node:fs/promises';
const base='http://127.0.0.1:24410',token=process.env.REPRO_TOKEN;
const json=async(p,b)=>{const r=await fetch(base+p,{method:b?'POST':'GET',headers:{authorization:`Bearer ${token}`,'content-type':'application/json'},...(b?{body:JSON.stringify(b)}:{})});const t=await r.text();assert.ok(r.ok,`${p}: ${r.status} ${t}`);return JSON.parse(t)};
const evidence=[],steps=[];
const commands=["set -e; printf 'c\\na\\nb\\n' > shell-input.txt; printf 'BASH_PATH='; readlink /proc/$$/exe; bash --version | head -n 1; printf 'HEAD='; head -n 1 shell-input.txt; printf 'TAIL='; tail -n 1 shell-input.txt; printf 'WC='; wc -l < shell-input.txt; printf 'SORT\\n'; sort shell-input.txt; printf 'GREP='; grep b shell-input.txt; printf 'SED\\n'; sed 's/b/B/' shell-input.txt",'windows_verification_missing_command_83971'];
let scenario=0;
const stub=createServer(async(req,res)=>{try{const chunks=[];for await(const c of req)chunks.push(c);const input=JSON.parse(Buffer.concat(chunks));const msgs=input.messages,ix=msgs.findLastIndex(m=>m.role==='user'),history=msgs.slice(ix+1),tools=history.filter(m=>m.role==='tool');evidence.push({scenario,input});let call;
if(!tools.length)call={name:'run_shell',arguments:{command:commands[scenario]}};
else {const last=String(tools.at(-1).content);let parsed;try{parsed=JSON.parse(last)}catch{};const id=parsed?.id??parsed?.execution?.id;if(id&&!/COMMAND_NOT_FOUND/.test(last)&&!/(?:completed|failed)/.test(last))call={name:'execution_status',arguments:{execution_id:id,wait_ms:10000}};}
const delta=call?{role:'assistant',tool_calls:[{index:0,id:`call_${Date.now()}`,type:'function',function:{name:call.name,arguments:JSON.stringify(call.arguments)}}]}:{role:'assistant',content:'Shell results received.'};res.writeHead(200,{'content-type':'text/event-stream'});for(const[d,f]of[[delta,null],[{},call?'tool_calls':'stop']])res.write(`data: ${JSON.stringify({id:'windows',object:'chat.completion.chunk',created:1,model:'windows',choices:[{index:0,delta:d,finish_reason:f}]})}\n\n`);res.end('data: [DONE]\n\n');}catch(e){res.writeHead(500);res.end(String(e))}});
await new Promise(r=>stub.listen(24420,'127.0.0.1',r));
try{const m=await json('/api/models',{name:'Windows isolated',model:'windows',apiToken:token,baseUrl:'http://127.0.0.1:24420/v1'}),p=await json('/api/projects',{name:'Windows shell validation '+Date.now()});
for(let attempt=0;attempt<30;attempt++){for(scenario=0;scenario<commands.length;scenario++){const s=await json(`/api/projects/${p.id}/sessions`,{title:`Windows shell ${scenario}`,modelId:m.id,approvalMode:'always_allow'}),run=await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/runs`,{content:`Execute Windows verification scenario ${scenario}`});let terminal;
for(let i=0;i<240;i++){const r=(await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/runs`)).find(x=>x.id===run.id);if(['completed','failed','cancelled'].includes(r.status)){terminal=r;break}await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,1000))}assert.ok(terminal,'Run timeout');assert.equal(terminal.status,'completed',JSON.stringify(terminal));
const activity=await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/agent-activity`),records=await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/execution-runs`);const logs=[];for(const ex of activity.executions??[]){let cursor=0;for(let n=0;n<100;n++){const page=await json(`/api/sessions/${s.id}/agent-activity/executions/${ex.id}/logs?cursor=${cursor}`);logs.push(page);if(!page.truncated||page.nextCursor===cursor)break;cursor=page.nextCursor}};evidence.push({scenario,session:s,terminal,activity,records,logs});const output=logs.flatMap(x=>x.chunks??[]).map(x=>x.text).join('').replaceAll('\r','');
if(scenario===0){assert.ok(records.some(x=>x.exitCode===0),'successful execution required');for(const pattern of [/BASH_PATH=\/usr\/bin\/bash/,/GNU bash/,/HEAD=c/,/TAIL=b/,/WC=\s*3/,/SORT\na\nb\nc/,/GREP=b/,/SED\nc\na\nB/])assert.match(output,pattern)}steps.push({scenario,status:'PASS',runId:run.id,sessionId:s.id});}
}}finally{await writeFile('repro-evidence.json',JSON.stringify({steps,evidence},null,2));stub.closeAllConnections();stub.close()}







```

</details>

## Actual result

Observed on upstream main at 2026-11-03T13:03:01Z. Session `142e4d7f-b3a7-45dc-8468-a19e279eaa6f`, run `0ff8def7-e4a5-43d0-bf5e-c109a7b3f0a7`.

Sanitized production stack trace:

```text
Error: EPERM: operation not permitted, rename
  <data>/session-runs/.state-37512-ae6a0cf6-4653-49d3-ae81-cfccb1e4f462.tmp
  -> <data>/session-runs/142e4d7f-b3a7-45dc-8468-a19e279eaa6f.json
    at async rename (node:internal/fs/promises:784:10)
    at async SessionStore.writeArray (<checkout>/services/api/dist/store.js:1829:13)
    at async <checkout>/services/api/dist/store.js:1840:13
errno: -4048
code: EPERM
syscall: rename
Node.js v22.23.4
```

The client subsequently fails with `ECONNREFUSED`; the API is no longer listening.

## Expected result

Transient Windows state-file replacement conflicts should be handled without terminating the API. If persistence cannot recover, the affected run should report an actionable error and the service should remain available, with run state recoverable.

## Investigation and limits

`services/api/src/store.ts` implements `writeArray` as temporary-file `writeFile` followed by a direct `rename`, with temporary-file cleanup in `finally`. Its mutation queue serializes writes to one path, but the replacement has no Windows retry behavior. Upstream already provides a retrying `renameReplace` in `services/api/src/platform/os/win32.ts` (related platform work: #637); this particular persistence path does not use it.

The exact conflicting file handle has **not** been identified. Antivirus, indexing, or overlapping readers are hypotheses, not established causes. The observed facts are the real `EPERM` replacement failure and the unhandled error terminating the production API. No product fix is included in this report.

