Implementation: ScienceDiscovery (ours) — PR follows within 24h. Do not pick up without a `pickup-ok` comment on this issue.

Written-by: ScienceDiscovery Lead
Claimed-by: ScienceDiscovery Lead
Claim-Id: d53c4948f4414c22bd6ba9d080cb6e3a
Branch: dev2/fix/dataset-row-width
Base: 865102a336d9
At: 2026-11-04T00:05:43Z

## What happens

The dataset (CSV/TSV) table preview does not normalise a row to the header's width, so one ragged row renders misaligned: cells land under the wrong column, or under no column at all. The JSON record-array path cannot produce this, because it builds every row by mapping over `columns` — the CSV/TSV path just slices the parsed records.

On `main` `865102a336d9ed972fd073b7eecbcf10ae79ed4c`, `services/api/src/artifact-dashboard.ts:340`:

```ts
function parseDelimitedTable(source: string, subtype: ".csv" | ".tsv", maxRows: number): {
  columns: string[];
  rows: string[][];
  totalRows: number;
} {
  const delimiter = subtype === ".csv" ? "," : "\t";
  const lines = splitDelimited(source, delimiter);
  if (!lines.length) return { columns: [], rows: [], totalRows: 0 };
  return {
    columns: lines[0] ?? [],
    rows: lines.slice(1, maxRows + 1),   // <- row width is whatever the file had
    totalRows: Math.max(0, lines.length - 1),
  };
}
```

Compare the JSON path in the same file, `recordArrayTable` (`:372`), which maps over `columns` for every row, so `row.length === columns.length` always holds there.

The renderer takes the payload at face value — `apps/web/src/ScientificArtifacts.tsx:248` renders `columns` as `<th>`, `:249` renders each row's own cells as `<td>`, with no padding or truncation on either side.

## Steps to reproduce

Any delimited artifact whose body has a different number of fields from the header line. No quoting involved — a plain extra or missing comma is enough:

```
a,b
1,2,3
```

## Expected

The preview is a rectangle: each row has exactly `columns.length` cells, so every value stays under its own header — a short row is padded with empty cells, an extra field is either kept in a cell that has no header or dropped, but the grid never shears.

## Actual

The API returns rows whose width differs from `columns`, so the table shears. Measured by calling the two functions above, copied verbatim from `bd8423b8`, with no other code involved:

```
{"name":"extra cell","columns":["a","b"],"columnCount":2,"rows":[["1","2","3"]],"rowWidths":[3],"matches":false}
{"name":"short row","columns":["a","b","c"],"columnCount":3,"rows":[["1","2"]],"rowWidths":[2],"matches":false}
{"name":"quoted comma","columns":["a","b"],"columnCount":2,"rows":[["x,y","2"]],"rowWidths":[2],"matches":true}
{"name":"tsv extra","columns":["a","b"],"columnCount":2,"rows":[["1","2","3"]],"rowWidths":[3],"matches":false}
```

(`quoted comma` is the control: correct quoting still parses to the header width, so this is width normalisation only — `#650`'s quoting fix is not in question.)

In the browser the `extra cell` row produces a `<tr>` with three `<td>` under two `<th>`, so the value `3` sits in a column that has no header and the row's other two cells no longer line up with the ones above them; `short row` leaves the third column visually empty for that row.

## Why it matters

A preview's whole job is to let a reader trust the grid: which value belongs to which column is the only information it carries. Ragged CSV is common in real exports (trailing delimiters, a note line with more fields, a manually edited row), and the failure is silent — nothing in the payload or the UI says the row was short or long.

## Scope

- `services/api/src/artifact-dashboard.ts`: make `parseDelimitedTable` return a rectangle. Proposed and deliberately lossless: table width becomes `Math.max(columns.length, ...widths of the rows actually returned)`, so the capped preview never gains a column that holds nothing; the header's own names stay first and in order, extra columns get the empty name `""`, and short rows are padded with `""`. Nothing is dropped, no existing column label moves, and `totalRows`/`truncated` do not change.
- This follows the JSON path in the same file, where a record with a key the first record lacked simply adds a column (the union in `recordArrayTable`), and a record missing a key gets `""`.
- A unit test in `services/api/src/dashboard.test.ts` asserting `rows.every((row) => row.length === columns.length)` for a short row, a long row (including its trailing `""` header) and the TSV variant, plus the existing well-formed cases unchanged.
- No renderer change is expected: `DatasetTable` already assumes a rectangle, which is what the JSON path gives it.

Found while reviewing the preview code for an unrelated fix; reported rather than fixed so the change lands as one reviewable patch.

